.th BASICP I June/77
.sh NAME
basicp \*- Basic-Plus
.sh SYNOPSIS
.bd basicp
.sh DESCRIPTION
This description is intended to supplement the Basic-plus Language Manual (DEC-11-ORBPA-C-D)
for UNIX users of Basic-plus.  Only changes to the manual will be noted here:
the references are to section numbers in the language manual.
.sp
1.1) Introduction
.sp
	Basic-plus is a subsystem under the Unix time-sharing monitor.
To run basic plus, type:
.sp
	% basicp
.sp
	The language implementation corresponds to RSTS/E version 5B.

 2.3.2) Typing conventions
.sp
	All lines are terminated by the RETURN or NEW-LINE key, as is usual
with Unix. Continuation lines are indicated by immediately preceeding the new-line with
an underscore (_). The interrupt
character is a rubout; this is usually intercepted by basic.
An end-of-file
is part of the information in a Unix file and is not indicated by a ^Z. An end-of-file
from the terminal may be inidcated by a ^D.
The pseudo-device \fIkb:\fR gives the standard input and output
for basic.


8.4)	System functions and statements

.sp
The rad$() function has been eliminated.
.sp
The wait option in terminal input is not yet supported.
.sp
A new statment has been added. The syntax is:
.sp
	(1)	shell
.br
or,
.br
	(2)	shell <string expression>

In form (1) the Unix shell is invoked until an end-of-file, when control
returns to basic. In form (2) the string expression is treated as a Unix command
and executed. Control remains inside of basic. Note that these may occur in
a program or in immediate mode.


9.1)	File storage
.sp
A Unix basic+ filename is a Unix pathname of 64 or fewer letters.
Program files must have an extension; ".bas" is the default.
Compiled files also must have an extension; ".bac" is the default.
Other than this, no extensions are used.
.sp
Problem names are limited to 10 characters. They are obtained in the
obvious manner from Unix pathnames.

9.2.1)	Recordsize option
.sp
Any even integer may be used as a recordsize.
The default is 512.

9.2.2)	Clustersizes
.sp
The clustersize option has been eliminated.

9.7)
.sp
Update mode does not exist.


11.8.1) Filesize
.sp
Filesize has been eliminated.
All subsections of (11.11) are invalid.


12) Device dependence

	All files behave as well-mannered Unix files.


13) sys() calls

.in 20
.ti 10
sys(chr$(0)+chr$(n))
Reset control-O on basic channel n.
If n is omitted, 0 is assumed.
Nothing of interest is returned.
.sp
.ti 10
sys(chr$(2)+chr$(n))
Enable echoing on basic channel n.
If n is omitted, 0 is assumed.
Nothing of interest is returned.
.sp
.ti 10
sys(chr$(3)+chr$(n))
Disable echoing on basic channel n.
If n is omitted, 0 is assumed.
Nothing of interest is returned.
.sp
.ti 10
sys(chr$(4)+chr$(n))
Next input operation on basic channel n is
in odt (raw) mode. If n is omitted, 0 is assumed.
Nothing of interest is returned.
The same cautions given in the RSTS manual for odt
mode apply.
Note that under UNIX an interrupt does not cause an
exit from a raw-mode input request.
.sp
.ti 10
sys(chr$(5))
Exit to the editor without a READY message
.sp
.ti 10
sys(chr$(6)+chr$(-7))
Enable interrupt error trap
.sp
.ti 10
sys(chr$(6)+chr$(9)+chr$(m))
Return error message text for error m.
If m is omitted, zero is assumed.
The first two bytes of the error message are null.
(In RSTS they were the job and keyboard numbers.)
.sp
.ti 10
sys(chr$(6)+chr$(60))
Exit to UNIX
.sp
.ti 10
sys(chr$(6)+chr$(61))
Fork. The child process receives a null string value;
the parent does not.
.sp
.ti 10
sys(chr$(7)+c$)
c$ is made the core-common string.
.sp
.ti 10
sys(chr$(8))
The core-common string is returned.
.sp
.ti 10
sys(chr$(9))
Exit to editor and set up Noname.
.sp 2
.sh FILES
/usr/lib/basic.errf
.sh DIAGNOSTICS
self-explanatory
.sh "SEE ALSO"
trbas(I)
.sh BUGS
